<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanagawa Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kanagawa Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the south-west side of Tokyo Bay, with its capital at Yokohama. It covers the former province of Sagami and part of Musashi, including the old castle town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a>, and was formed after the abolition of the domains in 1871. Its ports, above all Yokohama, were central to Japan's opening to foreign trade from 1859.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanagawa-prefecture.jpg" alt="Kanagawa Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dick Thomas Johnson from Tokyo, Japan · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yokohama_(52978652484).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kanagawa is a small prefecture at the south-eastern corner of the Kantō Plain, between Tokyo to the north, the foothills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-fuji">Mount Fuji</a> to the north-west, and Sagami Bay and Tokyo Bay to the south and east. It covers 2,415 square kilometres, and the Miura Peninsula separates the two bays. Topographically it has three areas: a mountainous west with the Tanzawa Mountain Range and the volcano Mount Hakone, a hilly east formed by the Tama Hills and the Miura Peninsula, and a central region of river terraces and low land along the Sagami, Sakai, Tsurumi and Tama rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological sites go back to the Jōmon period. The imperial dynasty is believed to have ruled the area from the 5th century onward, though in ancient times its plains were sparsely inhabited. In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> it belonged to the provinces of Sagami and Musashi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> in central Sagami was the seat of the Kamakura shogunate and the capital of Japan from 1185 to 1333. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the daimyō of Odawara Castle governed western Sagami, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> in Edo governed the eastern part directly.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Commodore Matthew Perry</a> landed in Kanagawa in 1853 and 1854 and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-kanagawa">Convention of Kanagawa</a>, which opened Japanese ports to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Yokohama, the largest deep-water port in Tokyo Bay, was opened to foreign traders in 1859 and grew into the largest trading port in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Yokosuka, nearer the mouth of the bay, developed as a naval port. The Meiji government built Japan's first railway, from Shinbashi in Tokyo to Yokohama, in 1872.</p><p>The Great Kantō earthquake of 1923 had its epicentre deep beneath Izu Ōshima in Sagami Bay. It devastated Yokohama and Tokyo; at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> the dead from earthquake, tsunami and fire exceeded 2,000, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> ninety percent of the buildings collapsed at once. American bombing in 1945 heavily damaged Yokohama, Kawasaki and other cities. After the war General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</a>, landed in Kanagawa. The prefecture's population was about 1.9 million in 1945 and grew rapidly as part of the Greater Tokyo Area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yokohama is the capital and largest city, with Kawasaki, Sagamihara and Fujisawa among the other major cities. Kanagawa has 9,221,129 inhabitants (1 April 2022), making it the second most populous prefecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and became so in 2006. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and Hakone are popular destinations for visitors from Tokyo, and Fuji-Hakone-Izu National Park lies partly within the prefecture. Shin-Yokohama and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> stations are on the Tōkaidō Shinkansen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kanagawa Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the abolition of the domains in 1871 it was absorbed into <b>Kanagawa Prefecture</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/odawara-domain">Odawara Domain</a></li></ul></section>
